Take the rewind snapshot inside the frame

retro_serialize() runs the emulation until the end of the frame it is
called in: the state is written at the first safe interrupt, then the
rest of that frame is emulated and never shown. A frontend that snapshots
between two frames, as a rewind does, pays a whole extra frame each time.
Three entry points queue the save for the next retro_run() instead, which
takes it at the same point and goes on with the frame it was going to run
anyway: retro_serialize_in_frame() queues it, _result() says when it has
landed (the core postpones a save while the RSP is mid-task), _cancel()
drops it. They are exported as n64_retro_* by Vendor/n64-ios/n64_symbols.h.

In the libretro build the state is also written straight into the
caller's buffer, which retro_serialize_size() sizes exactly, instead of
being built in a fresh 16 MB allocation and copied across. Same bytes,
one allocation, its page faults and a 16 MB pass fewer on every save.

Applied at build time by Vendor/n64-ios/build.sh, after 0007.

--- a/libretro/libretro.c
+++ b/libretro/libretro.c
@@ -2166,6 +2166,47 @@
     return 16788288 + 1024 + 4 + 4096;
 }
 
+/* A save taken INSIDE the next retro_run, for the frontend's rewind.
+ *
+ * retro_serialize() asks for a save, then runs the emulation until the
+ * frame it is in has ended: the state is written at the first safe interrupt,
+ * and the rest of that frame is emulated without being shown. Called between
+ * two retro_run(), that is one whole extra frame, every time. Here the job is
+ * only queued; the next retro_run() takes the state at the same point
+ * retro_serialize() would have (the end of the interrupt the core yielded
+ * in) and carries on with the frame it was going to run anyway.
+ *
+ * The save can land later than that frame: the core postpones it while the
+ * RSP is mid-task. retro_serialize_in_frame_result() says when it has landed;
+ * until then `data` belongs to the core and must not be read, freed or
+ * reused. Any retro_serialize(), retro_unserialize() or reset replaces the
+ * queued job, so the frontend cancels its own first. */
+RETRO_API bool retro_serialize_in_frame(void *data, size_t size)
+{
+   if (initializing || !data || size < retro_serialize_size())
+      return false;
+
+   retro_savestate_complete = false;
+   retro_savestate_result = 0;
+   savestates_set_job(savestates_job_save, savestates_type_m64p, data);
+   return true;
+}
+
+/* 0 while the save is queued, 1 once it is in the buffer, -1 if it failed. */
+RETRO_API int retro_serialize_in_frame_result(void)
+{
+   if (!retro_savestate_complete)
+      return 0;
+   return retro_savestate_result ? 1 : -1;
+}
+
+/* Drops a queued save that has not landed, so its buffer is free again. */
+RETRO_API void retro_serialize_in_frame_cancel(void)
+{
+   if (savestates_get_job() == savestates_job_save)
+      savestates_set_job(savestates_job_nothing, savestates_type_unknown, NULL);
+}
+
 bool retro_serialize(void *data, size_t size)
 {
    if (initializing)
